WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.800
Before you can analyse a runner's gait,
you need footage you can trust.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.100
Here's how to set up your cameras so every
assessment is consistent and repeatable.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.200
Film at least two views:
one from the side and one from behind.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.400
Record both sides, because asymmetries
are exactly what you're looking for.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.100
Add a front view if your space allows it.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.700
A smartphone filming at 60 frames per second
or more is all you need,

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.700
mounted at roughly hip height, far enough back
to see several full strides, and square to the runner.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.500
Even a slight angle distorts
what you see from behind.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.000
Before you film, mark the key landmarks
with a whiteboard marker or small stickers:

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.200
the ASIS and PSIS, greater trochanter, mid-thigh,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.100
lateral knee joint line, mid-tibia
and lateral malleolus.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.700
Treadmill or overground both work.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.300
Give the runner one to two minutes to settle into
a natural rhythm before you start recording.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.800
Keep shoes and fitted clothing
the same between sessions.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.200
Take note of the exact speed,
so a retest is comparable.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.100
Then capture at least 10 to 15 seconds
of continuous running for each view.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.000
If you plan to measure distances or angles later,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.400
include a reference measurement, like a measured stick
or piece of equipment, in the frame.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.200
Same set-up, every runner, every time.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.000
That consistency is what makes
your gait analysis reliable.
